<section>
    <h2>Recognising a request</h2>
    <div class="card-row">
        <div class="card fragment"><h4>Method</h4><p><code>GET</code>, <code>POST</code>…</p></div>
        <div class="card violet fragment"><h4>URL</h4><p>Absolute, query string included</p></div>
        <div class="card green fragment"><h4>Body</h4><p>When it is a string; a streamed body is not compared</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="fragment callout">
        Requests are first <b>normalised</b> the way HttpClient does it: <code>json</code>, <code>query</code>,
        <code>base_uri</code>, <code>auth_basic</code>, <code>auth_bearer</code>… end up in the recorded URL, body and headers.
    </p>
    <p class="fragment subtitle" style="font-size: 0.6em;">Secrets redacted on both sides, and the random boundary of a multipart body replaced with a fixed value.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>A request is recognised by three criteria.</p>
        <p>[click] The method: GET, POST, and so on.</p>
        <p>[click] The URL, absolute, query string included.</p>
        <p>[click] And the body, when it's a string. A streamed body isn't compared.</p>
        <p>[click] But before comparing, requests are normalised, exactly the way HttpClient does it. The json, query, base_uri, auth_basic and auth_bearer options end up in the recorded URL, body and headers. So whether you passed your parameters through the query option or directly in the URL, it's the same request.</p>
        <p>[click] Secrets are redacted on both sides. And the boundary of a multipart body, which is random, is replaced with a fixed value. Otherwise, no file upload would ever match.</p>
        <p>By the way: there was a MatcherInterface in the first versions. It was removed in the final rework. To ignore an unstable field, you go through redact. A customisable matcher may come back later, if a real need shows up.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
